userStories.description:
**Goal** — Add the generic graph contracts, engine-owned key constant, aggregate validator and public barrel without adding a runner or a production consumer.

**Motivation** — Steps that only make sense inside a nax run cannot be reused elsewhere.

**Approach** — A deterministic runner over a typed, validated graph value. No LLM, no I/O beyond logging: the runner calls only what the caller injects (node run functions, guards, the cost-stop hook, the hooks and the run options).

The engine needs nothing beyond `@/errors` (`NaxError`), `@/logger` (`getSafeLogger`) and `@/log-format` (`formatDuration`). It never imports from `src/workflow/`: G2 makes the workflow import the engine, and the reverse edge would close an import cycle that `check:import-cycles` counts.

`check:complexity` caps every function at cognitive complexity 20 (`STRICT_LIMIT`, `packages/repo-tooling/scripts/check-complexity.ts:74`), and a new file has no baseline entry. `runStepGraph` therefore stays a short loop over small helpers (for example: one iteration, patch checking, transition choice, the HEAD read), the way `runFeatureMachine` keeps its logic in `costStop`, `readHeadSoft` and `applyPatch`. The helper names are the builder's.

The engine has no precedent in nax, so the shape it must reach is given here. Exact generic spellings are the builder's; the names, fields and semantics are the contract.

**Interface** —
```ts
// src/step-graph/types.ts
export type StepKind = "agent" | "gate" | "git" | "forge" | "pure";

/** The engine-owned fields every graph record carries. */
export interface GraphState<N extends string> {
  readonly step: N;                                  // cursor: the next node to run, or an end id
  readonly timings: Readonly<Record<string, number>>; // ms per node id, accumulated
  readonly lastHead?: string;                        // HEAD after the last committing node
}

/** The record keys the engine writes itself; no node may declare or patch them. */
export const ENGINE_KEYS = ["step", "timings", "lastHead"] as const;

export interface StepResult<S> {
  readonly patch: Partial<S>;   // applied key by key; an `undefined` value deletes the key
  readonly exit?: string;       // optional label the transitions can match; never persisted
}

export interface StepTransition<S, E, N extends string> {
  readonly to: N;
  readonly on?: string;                              // matches StepResult.exit
  readonly when?: (state: S, env: E) => boolean;     // evaluated on the post-patch state
}

export interface StepNode<S, E, N extends string> {
  readonly kind: StepKind;
  readonly commits?: boolean;
  readonly needs?: readonly string[];
  readonly produces: readonly string[];
  readonly exits?: readonly string[];
  readonly transitions: readonly StepTransition<S, E, N>[];
  run(env: E, state: S): Promise<StepResult<S>>;
}

export interface CostStop<S, N extends string> {
  readonly patch: Partial<S>;
  readonly to: N;
}

export interface StepGraph<S extends GraphState<N>, E, N extends string> {
  readonly start: N;
  readonly end: readonly N[];
  readonly inputs?: readonly string[];               // keys the initial record supplies
  readonly nodes: Readonly<Partial<Record<N, StepNode<S, E, N>>>>;
  /** The keys a `costStop` patch may set; required when `costStop` is defined. */
  readonly costStopProduces?: readonly string[];
  /** Asked only for an agent node while the cost limit is exceeded; undefined = run the node. */
  costStop?(node: N, state: S, env: E): Promise<CostStop<S, N> | undefined>;
}

export interface StepGraphHooks<S, N extends string> {
  persist(state: S): Promise<void>;
  onStep?(node: N, state: S): void;
}

export interface StepGraphRunOptions<N extends string> {
  aborted(): boolean;
  now(): number;
  costExceeded?(): boolean;
  readHead?(): Promise<string | undefined>;
  onAbort?(node: N): Error;
  logScope?: string;                                 // set = the engine logs step lines (below)
}

// src/step-graph/validate.ts
export function validateStepGraph(graph): { errors: string[] };
export function defineStepGraph(graph): typeof graph; // throws NaxError STEP_GRAPH_INVALID
```

`src/step-graph/index.ts` is the barrel. It exports every name above; `runStepGraph` joins it in US-002.

**Approach — Validation rules (verbatim)**

`validateStepGraph(graph)` returns `{ errors }` and lists every violation, not only the first. Each message names the offending node, key or id:

1. `start` is a node id or an end id.
2. Every transition `to` is a node id or an end id.
3. Every node has at least one transition.
4. No end id is also a node id.
5. A transition's `on` is one of its node's `exits`.
6. Every key in a node's `needs` is an engine key, in `graph.inputs`, in some node's `produces`, or in `graph.costStopProduces`.
7. No node's `produces`, and not `graph.costStopProduces`, contains an engine key (`step`, `timings`, `lastHead`).
8. A graph with any `agent` node defines `costStop`, and a graph that defines `costStop` defines `costStopProduces` (an empty list counts as defined).

Every `STEP_GRAPH_*` error the engine throws carries `context.stage` `"step-graph"`.

`defineStepGraph(graph)` returns the graph unchanged when there are no errors. Otherwise it throws `NaxError` with code `STEP_GRAPH_INVALID`, `context.stage` `"step-graph"`, and a message listing every error. `runStepGraph` runs the same validation before it calls anything and throws the same error. The last sentence is the downstream US-002 seam, not an instruction to implement runStepGraph here.

**Integration notes** — `NaxError(message: string, code: string, context?: Record<string, unknown>)` — `@/errors` (re-exported from `@nathapp/nax-agent/internal`). Its `context` always carries `stage` (`.nax/rules/error-handling.md`). Validation checks declarations only and must never invoke run functions, guards or the cost-stop hook. Build unit fixtures with concrete graph/state types and use bun:test.

**Scope** — In: create `src/step-graph/types.ts`, `src/step-graph/validate.ts`, `src/step-graph/index.ts` and `test/unit/step-graph/validate.test.ts`. Out: implementing `runStepGraph` or exporting it before US-002; runtime cost-stop, HEAD and log policies owned by US-003; any change to existing nax modules or production wiring; workflow imports and G2/G3 ports. No dependencies; workdir `packages/nax`.

**Goal** — Extend the upstream runner with agent cost-stop substitution, fail-soft lastHead tracking and opt-in step log lines without disturbing its core callback and timing order.

**Motivation** — The engine has to reproduce `runFeatureMachine`'s loop exactly, so that the G2 port can pass the G0 golden traces (`test/fixtures/workflow-equivalence/`, merged in #2618).

**Approach** — A deterministic runner over a typed, validated graph value. No LLM, no I/O beyond logging: the runner calls only what the caller injects (node run functions, guards, the cost-stop hook, the hooks and the run options).

The engine needs nothing beyond `@/errors` (`NaxError`), `@/logger` (`getSafeLogger`) and `@/log-format` (`formatDuration`). It never imports from `src/workflow/`: G2 makes the workflow import the engine, and the reverse edge would close an import cycle that `check:import-cycles` counts.

`check:complexity` caps every function at cognitive complexity 20 (`STRICT_LIMIT`, `packages/repo-tooling/scripts/check-complexity.ts:74`), and a new file has no baseline entry. `runStepGraph` therefore stays a short loop over small helpers (for example: one iteration, patch checking, transition choice, the HEAD read), the way `runFeatureMachine` keeps its logic in `costStop`, `readHeadSoft` and `applyPatch`. The helper names are the builder's.

**Interface** — Reuse the upstream types and extend the existing function without changing its public signature. The relevant contracts are:
```ts
export interface CostStop<S, N extends string> {
  readonly patch: Partial<S>;
  readonly to: N;
}

export interface StepGraphRunOptions<N extends string> {
  aborted(): boolean;
  now(): number;
  costExceeded?(): boolean;
  readHead?(): Promise<string | undefined>;
  onAbort?(node: N): Error;
  logScope?: string;                                 // set = the engine logs step lines (below)
}

// src/step-graph/run.ts
export function runStepGraph(graph, env, state, hooks, options): Promise<S>;
```
The upstream StepGraph has `readonly costStopProduces?: readonly string[];` and `costStop?(node: N, state: S, env: E): Promise<CostStop<S, N> | undefined>;`. Import every type from `src/step-graph/types.ts`.

**Approach — Runner policy insertions (verbatim)**

6. Cost stop: only when the node's kind is `agent`, `options.costExceeded` is given and returns true, call `graph.costStop(node, state, env)`. A returned `{ patch, to }` replaces steps 7-8: the node's `run` is not called, the patch is applied, the cursor becomes `to`, and no HEAD is read. Every key of that patch must be in `graph.costStopProduces`, else throw `STEP_GRAPH_UNDECLARED_OUTPUT`. A `to` that is neither a node id nor an end id throws `STEP_GRAPH_UNKNOWN_NODE`; an end id is valid and ends the run at the next iteration. `undefined` means run the node.

9. If the node has `commits: true`, was not cost-stopped and `options.readHead` is given, `head = await options.readHead()`. A non-empty string replaces `lastHead`. `undefined` or an empty string keeps the previous `lastHead`, as `runFeatureMachine` does. A throw keeps the previous `lastHead` and logs one `warn` on stage `"step-graph"`: `Could not read HEAD after committing node <node>; the record keeps the last one`, with data `{ storyId: logScope ?? "_run", node, error: <the error's message> }`. This catch is a backstop. G2 passes the workflow's own `readHeadSoft`, which catches, logs the workflow's existing warning and resolves `undefined`, so the G0 goldens keep that warning.

Retain the core order from US-002: validate before anything; at each cursor check end, unknown node, abort; call onStep; take the first now read; insert step 6; for an ordinary execution validate run's declared outputs and exit, apply its patch and choose the first matching transition using post-patch guards; insert step 9; take the second now read, build accumulated timings and the next cursor; emit done/skipped if enabled; await persist. The final onStep(endId, state) still runs once. A cost-stopped node bypasses run and transitions, but still contributes elapsed time under its own id, persists its patched state and validates its destination before persisting.

A throw at any step leaves the loop at once. `persist` is not called for the node that threw, so the last persisted record still names that node as `step`, and a resume runs it again. A throw from `node.run`, `graph.costStop`, a guard or `hooks.persist` propagates unchanged. A persistence rejection propagates without continuing or retrying; readHead alone has the fail-soft catch above.

Every iteration calls `options.now()` exactly twice: once in step 5 and once in step 10. With `logScope` unset, the engine adds no `now()` call and logs nothing except the step 9 warning.

Every `STEP_GRAPH_*` error the engine throws carries `context.stage` `"step-graph"`. An unknown cost-stop destination also has context.node naming that destination, consistent with the unknown-node error. Check cost-stop patch keys even when their values are undefined. No node or cost-stop patch may change engine-owned keys because validation excludes those keys from produces declarations.

**Approach — Step log lines (verbatim)**

When `options.logScope` is set, the engine logs `info` lines on stage `"step-graph"` with data `{ storyId: logScope, step: node, ... }`:

- between step 6 and step 7, so only a node that actually runs: message `<node> start`;
- at step 10, before `persist`: message `<node>: done  <formatDuration(elapsed)>` with `data.durationMs = elapsed`;
- for a cost-stopped node, instead of both: `<node>: skipped (cost limit reached)  <formatDuration(elapsed)>` with `data.durationMs = elapsed`.

The durations reuse the two `now()` reads of steps 5 and 10.

These lines are for graphs that have no step lines of their own, such as the later `nax bot` graphs. The workflow's step functions write their own lines on stage `workflow` through `startStep` (`src/workflow/run-log.ts`), with per-step labels, joiners, model names and result text, and the G0 goldens pin them. G2 therefore leaves `logScope` unset, so a ported workflow run logs exactly what it logs today.

**Integration (verbatim)** — `getSafeLogger(): Logger | null` — `@/logger`. Calls are `info(stage, message, data)` / `warn(stage, message, data)`, and `storyId` must be the first data key (`check:logger-storyid`).

`formatDuration(durationMs: number): string` — `@/log-format`.

**Scope** — In: extend `src/step-graph/run.ts` created by US-002 and create `test/unit/step-graph/run-policies.test.ts`, a concern split from `run.test.ts` (`<module>-<concern>.test.ts`) so this story owns its policy tests. Keep all US-002 core behavior and order unchanged. Out: changing graph validation or public types, rewriting core transition semantics or replacing US-002 tests, any existing nax module changes, workflow imports, production wiring, G2/G3 ports and golden replay, a step budget or parallel nodes. Use injected readHead only, never Git I/O. Capture logger entries using the existing initLogger/addSink/resetLogger pattern with cleanup, and use counters/event arrays for exact timing and invocation order. Depends on US-002; workdir `packages/nax`.
